18.text:
The flag of Tonga consists of a solid red field with a white canton (upper hoist-side quarter) containing a red Greek cross. Model the digitized flag as an \(H \times W\) matrix \(M\) (with \(W = 2H\)) whose entries are the scalars \(a\) (red pixels) or \(b\) (white pixels).  

The geometry produces only three distinct row vectors in \(M\):  
- the constant vector of all \(a\)'s (all rows below the canton together with every row that intersects the horizontal arm of the cross);  
- the vector whose first \(C = H/2\) entries equal \(b\) and whose remaining entries equal \(a\) (canton rows that miss the cross);  
- the same vector as the preceding one except that a central block of entries inside the canton equals \(a\) (canton rows that intersect the vertical arm of the cross).  

Hence \(\operatorname{rank}(M) \le 3\).  For generic distinct scalars \(a \ne b\) the three vectors are linearly independent, so the bound is attained.  No choice of \(a,b\) can raise the rank above three, because that would require a fourth distinct row, which the flag's geometry never supplies.  

Thus the maximal possible rank is exactly 3.  

PUBLIC RESPONSE: {3}

